# Lock of the local `erotica-2026-10` conda environment (osx-arm64, Python 3.14.10), the default
# environment for EROTICA work since 2026-10-26. Recorded 2026-11-05.
#
# THREE FILES
#   requirements.in          `pip freeze` of the environment, minus two lines that are not on PyPI:
#                            the editable `erotica` itself and `ellc` (see below).
#   requirements.lock.txt    `uv pip compile` of requirements.in constrained by itself, so it pins
#                            exactly what is installed. Checked against the freeze: same 162 names
#                            and versions, the only differences being PEP 503 spelling
#                            (`jaraco.classes` -> `jaraco-classes`, `kepler.py` -> `kepler-py`).
#   conda-explicit.txt       `conda list --explicit --md5`: the conda layer (python, compilers,
#                            gfortran used to build ellc). Everything else is pip.
#
# REGENERATE (MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=14.0 because torch 2.14.1 ships only macosx_14_0_arm64
# wheels; uv's default target is 13.0 and the solve fails without it)
#   ~/miniforge3/envs/erotica-2026-10/bin/python -m pip freeze | grep -vE '^ellc @|^-e ' > requirements.in
#   MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=14.0 uv pip compile requirements.in -c requirements.in \
#       --python-version 3.14.10 --python-platform aarch64-apple-darwin --no-annotate -o requirements.lock.txt
#   conda list --explicit --md5 -p ~/miniforge3/envs/erotica-2026-10 > conda-explicit.txt
#
# RECREATE
#   conda create -n erotica-2026-10 --file conda-explicit.txt
#   uv pip install --python <env>/bin/python -r requirements.lock.txt
#   pip install -e <erotica checkout> --no-deps
#   ellc 1.8.15: the PyPI wheel/sdist carries an x86_64 libellc.so; the arm64 build is from the
#   sdist with conda-forge gfortran and the patch of pmaxted/ellc#30. Nothing in the `erotica`
#   package or its tests imports ellc; only the HD 159176 scripts of the hub do.
#
# No hashes: `--generate-hashes` would list every platform's wheel for each pin. This is a record
# of one machine's environment, not a cross-platform install spec; environment.yml stays the
# source of truth for dependencies.
